WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.839
OK, now that we've gone over all of the different things that we need to get the system working,

2
00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:12.279
as well as some of the prep work and things to consider before jumping into the engine,

3
00:00:12.279 --> 00:00:15.919
let's start to create some of the assets that we'll need to utilise in the vehicle's

4
00:00:15.919 --> 00:00:17.339
blueprint.

5
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:20.329
Now, we'll create two things first.

6
00:00:20.329 --> 00:00:23.969
We'll create the wheel blueprints for each type of wheel on our vehicle, so there'll

7
00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:29.010
be a front wheel and a rear wheel, and we'll create the curve asset that represents

8
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.949
the torque curve of our vehicle's engine.

9
00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:31.949
So those two things.

10
00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:36.799
But first, let's kick off with the wheel blueprints.

11
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:41.500
So the wheel blueprint does a lot of the heavy lifting when it comes to the initial setup

12
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.079
of the handling of the vehicle, and where you'll configure the settings for the wheels,

13
00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:50.579
the suspension and the brakes to ensure that things operate correctly.

14
00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:54.179
And you've got control over the setting of the wheel radius, the mass, the width,

15
00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:57.780
the handbrake effect, suspension, and many other properties to give your vehicle the

16
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.539
handling that you desire.

17
00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:05.959
With a four-wheeled vehicle, you typically need two different types of wheel blueprints.

18
00:01:05.959 --> 00:01:09.099
So one for the front wheels and one for the rear wheels.

19
00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:12.579
And you're setting up things like which ones are affected by the steering, which

20
00:01:12.579 --> 00:01:16.879
ones are affected by the engine and the handbrake and which ones aren't.

21
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:20.659
And because there's no limit to the number of wheels that a vehicle can have, that

22
00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:24.400
means that you can have as many wheel blueprints as you need, depending on the needs

23
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.659
of your vehicle.

24
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:30.340
And because the wheel blueprints are assets within the engine, and they're not just a

25
00:01:30.379 --> 00:01:34.879
setting within the vehicle blueprint itself, you can actually share them across all of

26
00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:37.900
the different vehicle blueprints in your project.

27
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:43.260
That is, if they share the same wheel dimensions and suspension limits, and essentially

28
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.549
a lot of the same settings that you can configure within the wheel blueprint.

29
00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:52.810
So it's about time we jumped into the engine and started creating the assets that we need

30
00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.579
for our Chaos Physics vehicle.

31
00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:59.500
But first, before we start creating any of these assets, we need to make sure that the

32
00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:01.739
plugin is enabled.

33
00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:06.280
So if you come up to edit on the top left here, down to plugins, this will open up your

34
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.500
plugins window.

35
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.949
And in the search box, if we look for chaos vehicles, here we go, we have a few options.

36
00:02:14.949 --> 00:02:19.009
There's the modular vehicles that we talked about and compared in the slides a little

37
00:02:19.009 --> 00:02:20.009
earlier on.

38
00:02:20.009 --> 00:02:23.229
But the one we're going to look at today is a chaos vehicles plugin, the standard

39
00:02:23.229 --> 00:02:25.050
chaos vehicles plugin.

40
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:28.389
So make sure you check this box, you should get a little option down here that says

41
00:02:28.389 --> 00:02:31.409
restart, and that'll just reload your project.

42
00:02:31.409 --> 00:02:36.310
And when it opens up again, the plugin will be loaded and ready to use.

43
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.830
Now that the plugin is active, you know, we know that the system is online.

44
00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.590
Let's start by making our wheel blueprints.

45
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.930
So we're going to need to make a wheel blueprint for the front wheels, and one

46
00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.879
for the rear wheels.

47
00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:52.240
So inside our content browser here, I'm just going to go into the vehicles folder and

48
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.939
create a new folder.

49
00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:59.770
And I'll just call this blueprints.

50
00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.250
And inside here, I'm pretty much going to build everything that we're going to need

51
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.719
to piece together to make this chaos vehicle of ours.

52
00:03:07.719 --> 00:03:14.080
So I'm going to right click inside the content browser and choose blueprint class.

53
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.060
And we come to the old classes drop down here.

54
00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.039
If this isn't open, you can just hit the little chevron to open it up.

55
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:26.520
And I'm going to search for wheel and then choose chaos vehicle wheel as our parent

56
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.479
class.

57
00:03:29.479 --> 00:03:34.860
And I'm going to call this BP underscore wheel underscore front, because this is going to

58
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.069
be the settings for the front wheel.

59
00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:42.349
And inside here, you've got all of the different properties you can configure for

60
00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:46.199
the wheel and for the suspension and for the brakes down here.

61
00:03:46.199 --> 00:03:50.719
And now you can set this up the axle type, whether it's front or rear, you can set

62
00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:56.840
the wheel width and radius and mass settings and any sort of coring stiffness and friction.

63
00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:02.439
So there's a lot you can do here with your initial setup to tweak sort of how the vehicle

64
00:04:02.439 --> 00:04:08.719
performs and handles, which we'll take a look at a little later on in the in the course,

65
00:04:08.719 --> 00:04:13.460
to give you like a little kickoff point for tweaking the settings for your own vehicles.

66
00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.500
But for now, there are six properties that we want to change, depending whether it's

67
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.740
the front or the back wheel and whether or not we want it to be affected by the engine

68
00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.939
or the brakes or the steering.

69
00:04:22.939 --> 00:04:24.899
So let's take a look at that now.

70
00:04:24.899 --> 00:04:28.540
So under the axle type, because we're working with the front wheel, we're going to set

71
00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.629
this to front.

72
00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:32.350
And we're going to set our wheel radius.

73
00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:37.329
Now, we've measured as previously, we know that it's 35.

74
00:04:37.329 --> 00:04:40.870
But if you remember from the slides a few moments ago, like is a great idea to just

75
00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:46.870
get the radius of your wheel or the diameter and then half it to figure out the setting

76
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.009
that you would need here.

77
00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:54.250
And then we need to figure out is this affected by the handbrake, which we're going

78
00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.170
to say no, because this is the front wheel.

79
00:04:57.170 --> 00:04:58.569
Is it affected by the engine?

80
00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:00.399
Again, no.

81
00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:01.680
And is it affected by steering?

82
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.759
And in this case, yes.

83
00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:08.040
And we're also going to tweak the max steering angle, because we find that 45 works pretty

84
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.610
well for this vehicle mesh that we have.

85
00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.279
And just come back to our content browser here.

86
00:05:16.279 --> 00:05:20.220
What I'm going to do for the rear wheel is just highlight the front wheel, press

87
00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:27.790
Ctrl D to duplicate it and rename it rear.

88
00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.750
And if I open that up, we just need to tweak some of the settings again.

89
00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:35.699
So the axle type this time is going to be rear, our wheel radius is the same.

90
00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:37.540
We do want it affected by the handbrake.

91
00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.500
And we do want it affected by the engine.

92
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:45.220
But this time, we don't want it to be affected by the steering.

93
00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:50.110
So if I compile and save that again, that's just finished with the first couple of

94
00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.870
assets that we're going to need to begin our chaos vehicle system.

95
00:05:55.870 --> 00:06:00.670
Now the second asset that we need to configure for the system is a curve asset.

96
00:06:00.709 --> 00:06:03.810
And this will represent the engine's torque curve.

97
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.050
Now as you know, this torque curve represents the amount of torque output in

98
00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:13.060
Newton meters from the engine at a given RPM.

99
00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:17.470
And this will be used to drive the acceleration of our vehicle.

100
00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.990
Right clicking inside our content browser, we'll come up to a miscellaneous here,

101
00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.180
and then choose curve.

102
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:29.959
And the class of curves that we want is a curve float.

103
00:06:29.959 --> 00:06:34.240
And I'm just going to call that torque curve.

104
00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:37.720
If I double click that to open it up, just dock it in here again,

105
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.360
you can see we get this graph with two axes, a vertical axis and a horizontal axis.

106
00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.740
And this is where we're going to create our curve.

107
00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:49.939
So the first thing that we want to do is you can see just as my mouse hovers

108
00:06:49.939 --> 00:06:54.899
over the zero line on the horizontal axis, you can see that everything sort

109
00:06:54.899 --> 00:06:55.579
of highlights.

110
00:06:55.579 --> 00:06:59.819
And this is where our curve is right now, essentially, except we don't have any

111
00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:03.670
keys on there to make it look any different than just a flat line.

112
00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.949
So anywhere along this curve, I'm just going to middle mouse click twice,

113
00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:09.829
once on the left, once on the right.

114
00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:13.990
And these are just going to represent the minimum and the maximum of our

115
00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:16.639
engine's RPM.

116
00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:20.920
So on the left hand side here, I'm going to set the horizontal axis to

117
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:27.930
zero, and the vertical axis to 0.5.

118
00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:32.410
And on the right hand side, we're going to set the horizontal axis to 5,000

119
00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:40.300
because that'll be our max RPM, and the vertical axis to 0.5.

120
00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:44.139
And if I just click inside the graph anywhere in an empty space and press

121
00:07:44.139 --> 00:07:49.709
this button here, or press F on the keyboard, what that's going to do is

122
00:07:49.709 --> 00:07:55.319
zoom out so that we can see all of the keys on our curve.

123
00:07:55.319 --> 00:07:58.519
Now, this is the beginning of our curve, but again, it's still very flat.

124
00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:02.379
So I'm just going to add two more keys.

125
00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:07.660
And for this first key, we'll set at 1,000 RPM.

126
00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:17.740
We want that to hit 100% of our torque value.

127
00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:27.230
And over here at 4,000 RPM, we want it to drop down to roughly 0.8,

128
00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:33.899
so about 80% of our torque value.

129
00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:38.299
So you'll notice that I haven't put the torque right up to any sort of crazy

130
00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:42.419
number because the value is actually normalized between zero and one.

131
00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:46.500
So one will essentially just give you whatever your maximum torque setting

132
00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.179
is at this RPM.

133
00:08:50.179 --> 00:08:52.740
So we don't actually put the torque values in here.

134
00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.830
They go into the vehicle's blueprint itself.

135
00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.110
But that's all we need to do to get our torque curve set up.

136
00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:02.190
This can be as complex or as simple as you'd like it to be.

137
00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:06.710
You can also smooth off these curves here by changing the key type

138
00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:07.509
that you have here.

139
00:09:07.509 --> 00:09:10.549
So you could right click, change it to any of these different sort

140
00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:11.269
of key types.

141
00:09:11.269 --> 00:09:15.190
So you could say auto and sort of smooth out all of these.

142
00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:18.950
You've also got the options across the top of the bar here, the toolbar

143
00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.379
just up here.

144
00:09:20.379 --> 00:09:23.990
But for now, we're going to just keep them all as linear.

145
00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:31.009
And if I save that and close it, we need to take a look at just one

146
00:09:31.009 --> 00:09:34.649
more asset before beginning to connect things up into our main vehicle

147
00:09:34.649 --> 00:09:37.480
blueprint.

148
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.120
And that is the vehicle's physics asset, now namely the one that was generated

149
00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.950
when we imported the skeletal mesh.

150
00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:49.470
Now, the collision bodies inside the physics asset get set up and generated

151
00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:53.830
automatically on import to the system's best guess, basically, as long

152
00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:55.889
as you have the option checked.

153
00:09:55.889 --> 00:09:59.289
Now, we want to delete all of this hard work that the engine's done for

154
00:09:59.289 --> 00:10:01.809
us and set them up manually.

155
00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:05.889
But we're just going to add physics bodies for the bones that we need so

156
00:10:05.889 --> 00:10:08.330
that we know it's going to work properly with the system that we're

157
00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.899
trying to create.

158
00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:16.539
So having said all that, if we find our existing physics asset, so come

159
00:10:16.539 --> 00:10:22.500
back to the vehicles folder here, into CSSUV and inside meshes, there's

160
00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.460
our skeletal mesh and our paired physics asset.

161
00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:31.139
If I double click to open this up, now when we open the physics asset,

162
00:10:31.139 --> 00:10:35.379
it's going to look something like this, where it's made its best guess

163
00:10:35.379 --> 00:10:42.259
at generating essentially all the collision bodies for all of the joints

164
00:10:42.259 --> 00:10:50.429
that are skinned in the skeletal mesh.

165
00:10:50.429 --> 00:10:53.830
But we don't really want any of these here because they're not the

166
00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.470
right shape, they're not the right size.

167
00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:58.950
There's too many of them for what we need.

168
00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:02.440
So I'm just going to highlight them all and press delete.

169
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.629
And then inside the skeleton tree here, I'm going to choose show

170
00:11:06.629 --> 00:11:09.629
weighted bones, and this will just show us the bones that have

171
00:11:09.629 --> 00:11:11.669
meshes weighted to them.

172
00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:15.070
And really what we're looking for ideally is the four wheel bones and

173
00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.149
the root.

174
00:11:16.149 --> 00:11:22.139
So we have the root here and we have the wheel rear and the wheel front.

175
00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:24.940
So first of all, for the root with the root highlighted, I'm going

176
00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:28.100
to come over to the tools panel just on the right hand side here

177
00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:31.610
and change the primitive type to be a box.

178
00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:36.169
And I'm going to untick orient, auto orient to bone, just so that

179
00:11:36.169 --> 00:11:39.409
when I hit add bodies now, we essentially just get a world

180
00:11:39.409 --> 00:11:43.649
aligned box that covers the entire sort of main body of the

181
00:11:43.649 --> 00:11:45.809
mesh here.

182
00:11:45.809 --> 00:11:48.750
And really, I'm just going to leave that as it is.

183
00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:52.029
And then for all of the wheels, if I highlight each of those,

184
00:11:52.029 --> 00:11:55.029
I'll turn back on orient to bone.

185
00:11:55.029 --> 00:11:59.409
And I'm going to change the primitive type to a sphere.

186
00:11:59.409 --> 00:12:01.750
And again, add bodies.

187
00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:05.389
What this does is it orients this sphere to the bone.

188
00:12:05.389 --> 00:12:08.629
And it does its best guess of encapsulating that mesh that is

189
00:12:08.669 --> 00:12:13.120
skinned to this bone within the physics body itself.

190
00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.259
And it's done a pretty good job at that straight away.

191
00:12:17.259 --> 00:12:19.379
And that's the main bulk of what we need to do to generate

192
00:12:19.379 --> 00:12:21.059
the physics bodies for this mesh.

193
00:12:21.059 --> 00:12:24.299
But now we need to just tweak some of the physics types

194
00:12:24.299 --> 00:12:28.379
and collision responses of the physics bodies for the

195
00:12:28.379 --> 00:12:31.019
wheels specifically, so that they react properly

196
00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:33.309
for our system.

197
00:12:33.309 --> 00:12:37.309
So if I select our wheel physics bodies here in the

198
00:12:37.309 --> 00:12:40.419
skeleton tree, and then come over to the details panel,

199
00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.659
I'll set the physics type to be kinematic.

200
00:12:43.659 --> 00:12:46.820
And all this does is make sure that the wheels are

201
00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.860
affected by the vehicle's bounds and shadows and

202
00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.110
culling occur correctly.

203
00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.190
And if I scroll a little further down,

204
00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:57.629
we can change the collision response to disabled,

205
00:12:57.629 --> 00:12:59.509
which means they have no collision on them.

206
00:12:59.509 --> 00:13:02.110
And that's just because these won't be used for

207
00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:03.830
collision for our vehicle.

208
00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:07.309
And instead the wheels use ray casting to interact with

209
00:13:07.309 --> 00:13:10.950
the world.

210
00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:12.870
Now we have our wheel blueprints.

211
00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.909
We have our torque curve, and we've updated the collision

212
00:13:16.909 --> 00:13:19.419
bodies in our physics asset.

213
00:13:19.419 --> 00:13:21.820
The final thing that we need to set up before we connect

214
00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:26.000
all of these things together is the animation blueprint.

215
00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.200
Now this is what we use to control skeletal mesh

216
00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:31.840
animations, typically an animation blueprint.

217
00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.519
So for us, that would be things like the tires spinning,

218
00:13:34.519 --> 00:13:37.779
the suspension, the steering animations.

219
00:13:37.779 --> 00:13:41.019
Now, normally you would create these animations sort of in

220
00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.299
external software or inside the engine itself,

221
00:13:44.299 --> 00:13:47.299
and then set up the logic to determine, you know,

222
00:13:47.299 --> 00:13:51.220
when these animations should play based on things like the

223
00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:53.700
skeletal meshes speed or direction,

224
00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:57.289
or if they're on or off the ground, these kinds of things.

225
00:13:57.289 --> 00:14:01.769
But something super useful in the chaos vehicle system is

226
00:14:01.769 --> 00:14:05.009
a single node that can get all of the information about

227
00:14:05.009 --> 00:14:08.169
the car's current state from the wheels.

228
00:14:08.169 --> 00:14:10.929
Things like how fast are they spinning or what are the

229
00:14:10.929 --> 00:14:13.129
suspension settings for this wheel?

230
00:14:13.129 --> 00:14:16.889
And then translate this information into bone animation

231
00:14:16.889 --> 00:14:20.049
for each of the associated wheels.

232
00:14:20.049 --> 00:14:22.649
Now, this means that it will be creating the bone

233
00:14:22.649 --> 00:14:26.289
animations at runtime without the need for us to set up

234
00:14:26.289 --> 00:14:29.929
any sort of complex logic or author any animations for

235
00:14:29.929 --> 00:14:33.059
when this car is driving around.

236
00:14:33.059 --> 00:14:35.740
And this node is the wheel controller.

237
00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.700
And it essentially queries information about the wheel,

238
00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.259
and then translates that into skeletal animation for the

239
00:14:42.259 --> 00:14:45.779
vehicle, saving us from creating animations and setting up

240
00:14:45.779 --> 00:14:48.419
a lot of complex logic.

241
00:14:48.419 --> 00:14:50.019
So back inside the engine,

242
00:14:50.019 --> 00:14:52.220
let's set up the animation blueprint.

243
00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:53.700
If I right click,

244
00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:56.980
come up to animation and choose animation blueprint,

245
00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:58.620
we choose what skeleton we want to affect.

246
00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:01.299
So that's our vehicle skeleton that we have here.

247
00:15:01.299 --> 00:15:03.980
And I'm going to choose a parent class.

248
00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.700
And this is going to be the vehicle animation instance.

249
00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:14.549
And if I rename our animation blueprint here

250
00:15:14.549 --> 00:15:17.100
to ABP chaos vehicle,

251
00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.809
I'll just double click that to open up.

252
00:15:19.809 --> 00:15:20.649
And inside here,

253
00:15:20.649 --> 00:15:24.580
we're only going to need a few nodes really

254
00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:25.980
that we're working with.

255
00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.179
So if I right click and search for the wheel controller

256
00:15:29.179 --> 00:15:30.980
for wheeled vehicle node,

257
00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:32.740
that was the one we just looked at,

258
00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.899
and connect that straight up to our output pose.

259
00:15:35.899 --> 00:15:37.539
Now this gives us a new node,

260
00:15:37.539 --> 00:15:39.580
a component to local mode,

261
00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:42.899
which essentially converts all of the translation information

262
00:15:42.899 --> 00:15:44.299
from component to local space,

263
00:15:44.299 --> 00:15:48.720
because the wheel controller runs in component space.

264
00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:50.519
And the last thing we're going to need

265
00:15:50.519 --> 00:15:55.820
is a mesh space ref pose.

266
00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:57.620
And I can create that by either dragging off

267
00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.659
the input pose or the wheel controller,

268
00:16:00.659 --> 00:16:03.340
or just right clicking and then searching for it

269
00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:06.000
and connecting the logical.

270
00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.639
And what this essentially does is grabs our meshes

271
00:16:09.639 --> 00:16:10.480
default pose,

272
00:16:10.519 --> 00:16:12.279
or what would be like a T pose for the character

273
00:16:12.279 --> 00:16:14.879
and says, hey, this is your starting point

274
00:16:14.879 --> 00:16:17.480
and for the wheel control to make its calculations

275
00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:20.539
from for the different transforms for the wheels.

276
00:16:20.539 --> 00:16:23.100
So we're grabbing our default pose.

277
00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.740
Then we're grabbing the information about the wheels,

278
00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.940
converting that from component space to local space

279
00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.600
and connecting that to our output pose.

280
00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:33.840
And that's all we need to do

281
00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:35.639
to set up the animation blueprint.
